※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A reference is only worth having while what it names is still there, so a
※ function handing one back says how long that is.  There are two answers and
※ the language has a word for each: `static` in the type says "as long as the
※ program", and `from v` after the type says "as long as what the parameter `v`
※ named".  Nothing else can be said, and nothing may be left unsaid.
※
※ **`static` belongs to the type, `from` to the signature.**  A lifetime that is
※ fixed once and for all is a property of the reference itself, so it is written
※ where the rest of the type is: `&mut static u8`.  A lifetime borrowed from a
※ parameter is a relation between two things in the signature, and no type could
※ name a parameter, so it is written after the type, once, for the whole answer.
※
※ **The caller works out the rest.**  A function promises no more than its
※ parameter's lifetime, so `first(&total)` -- where `total` is a variable at the
※ top level -- answers with a reference that lasts as long as the program, while
※ `first(&n)` for a local answers with one that does not.  The same function,
※ two answers, decided where both are in view.
※
※ Rust writes both of these as named lifetime parameters, `fn first<'a>(v: &'a
※ u8) -> &'a u8` and `&'static u8`, which say more -- several lifetimes at once,
※ and relations between them -- at the cost of a name to invent at every
※ signature.  Here a function answers with at most one reference, so the name
※ would only ever have one thing to point at, and pointing at the parameter
※ directly says the same thing with nothing to invent.  C++ has no such rule and
※ a dangling reference is a program nobody notices is wrong; Go and Java move
※ what escapes to the heap instead, which needs a collector.

let total: mut u8 = 0u8
let other: mut u8 = 0u8

※ As long as the program: what it names is a variable at the top level, which is
※ there before the first call and after the last.
fn counter() → &mut static u8:
    &mut total

※ As long as what `v` named, which is all this can promise and all a caller
※ needs: it hands back what it was given.
fn first(v: &u8) → &u8 from v:
    v

※ Which parameter, out of several, is exactly what `from` is for.
fn second(p: &u8, q: &u8) → &u8 from q:
    q

※ A reference handed on through another call keeps the promise it was made
※ with, so the two signatures agree with nothing written between them.
fn again(v: &u8) → &u8 from v:
    first(v)

@[impure]
fn bump(at: &mut u8) → &mut u8 from at:
    at⌖ ← at⌖ + 1u8
    at

※ A promise about the program's whole life is kept by anything that lasts that
※ long, so answering `from v` with a variable at the top level is allowed.
fn ignoring(v: &u8) → &u8 from v:
    &other

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    ※ a reference that lasts as long as the program, written through
    let c: &mut static u8 = counter()
    c⌖ ← 5u8
    ※ and standing where one with less to promise is wanted, which it may
    let shorter: &mut u8 = counter()
    shorter⌖ ← shorter⌖ + 1u8
    ※ borrowed from a local, which lives as long as the body
    let n: u8 = 7u8
    let r: &u8 = first(&n)
    let again_r: &u8 = again(&n)
    ※ borrowed from the second of two
    let x: u8 = 1u8
    let y: u8 = 2u8
    let q: &u8 = second(&x, &y)
    ※ the same function called with a top-level variable answers with one that
    ※ lasts as long as the program, which is what the type here demands
    let lasting: &static u8 = first(&total)
    ※ handed on and written through
    let m: mut u8 = 40u8
    let b: &mut u8 = bump(&mut m)
    b⌖ ← b⌖ + 1u8
    if total ≠ 6u8:
        1u6
    elif r⌖ ≠ 7u8 ∨ again_r⌖ ≠ 7u8:
        2u6
    elif q⌖ ≠ 2u8:
        3u6
    elif lasting⌖ ≠ 6u8:
        4u6
    elif m ≠ 42u8:
        5u6
    elif ignoring(&n)⌖ ≠ 0u8:
        6u6
    else:
        0u6
